WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.240
- So when I first got
into self-improvement,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.510
I had really bad social anxiety.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.800
Talking to somebody I
didn't know very well,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.750
especially if they were a girl,
was really difficult for me.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.170
Even carrying on a basic
human conversation was

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.420
something that I didn't really
know how to do very well.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.710
I'd always be stuck in
my head thinking about

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.130
what I was gonna say next,

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.610
and worrying about whether
they were impressed with me.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.360
And I also didn't have very many friends.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.310
People didn't really invite me anywhere.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.370
So I started searching on
Reddit for answers, you know,

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.240
kind of lurking through
self-improvement subreddits

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.410
like I used to do quite
a bit back in the day.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.340
And there was a book recommendation
that kept on coming up

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.690
in every single self-improvement
subreddit that I looked at.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.130
It seemed to be everyone's go-to book

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.250
they would recommend for people struggling

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.150
with social anxiety or
struggling with being able

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.470
to maintain a basic conversation.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:56.040
And that book was "How to Win
Friends and Influence People"

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.340
by Dale Carnegie.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.470
It came as kind of a shock
to me because this book was

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.920
published in 1936.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.010
In 2019, it's closer to the year 2100

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.210
than it is to when that
book was published,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.050
yet in 2011, TIME magazine ranked it

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:16.500
as the 19th most influential
book in American history.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.810
So it doesn't mess around.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.310
So I was starting to get
this vibe that it was

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.260
the bible of human communication,

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.630
and I gave it a read
and it changed my life.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.860
I'm not saying that this
book was responsible

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.930
for everything good that
ever happened in my life

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.040
or anything like that.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.780
But what I do mean is that
it changed my perspective.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.750
And I learned some things from this book

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.510
that I have carried with me to this day,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.290
I've never forgotten and
they've completely transformed

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.400
the way I interact with people,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.850
having conversations
with pretty much anybody

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.240
is not an issue for me anymore,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.190
and people actually invite me to stuff.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.970
So I'm gonna go over some of
the most impactful lessons

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.560
from the book, "How to Win
Friends and Influence People"

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.720
and hopefully it helps you out
as much as it helped me out.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.599
Just to preface before
I dive into these tips,

00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:08.200
or these lessons or whatever
you want to call them,

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.190
they are just tools.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.190
All these things I'm about
to say are just tools.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.490
They're not absolute
rules. They can be broken.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.950
But that being said, they
are very powerful tools

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.320
that I think work extremely
well in most situations,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.810
especially this first one
that I'm about to dive into.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.720
It's less of a tip or a tool,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.660
it's more like a paradigm shift

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.060
that you can apply to pretty
much your entire philosophy

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.470
when it comes to dealing with people.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.560
And that is lesson one,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.530
it's all about the other person.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.010
It may come as no surprise to you

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.320
that everyone basically just
thinks about themselves.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.370
What they've got going on in their lives,

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.740
what they want to eat for dinner,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.080
what are they stressing about,

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.330
what they want to do tomorrow,

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.530
the vacation they want
to take next summer.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.860
They're not really
thinking about you at all.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.430
You probably wouldn't be surprised

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.380
to find out that you primarily
think about yourself as well.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.810
And when you think about other people,

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.700
it's always in the context of you.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.660
It's like, "Does this person like me?

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.160
Do they think I'm stupid
because I said this thing?"

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.540
It's always about you.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.850
Everyone else thinks that exact same way.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.530
So in order to become better with people,

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.060
you have to realize this

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.900
and see things from other
people's perspective.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.620
People's favorite hobby and
people's favorite topic,

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.650
and the one they're most
comfortable talking about

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.560
is things to do with themselves.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.660
So if you want to become
a good conversationalist,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.470
you have to become an
investigator in people,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.060
try to get them talking about

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.540
what's going on in their lives

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.900
and you will never run
out of conversation,

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.530
which people sort of know,

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.640
but they don't practice
it because they feel like

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.270
they're not getting anything
out of the interaction,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.410
you know, by entirely focusing
on what the other person

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.080
has going on in their life, you know,

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.110
you're almost stepping on
yourself and not allowing

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.910
your own personality to
shine or something like that.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.330
But I found kind of the opposite is true.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.260
When you become genuinely
interested in other people

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.080
and what they have going on in their life,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.270
they're a lot more likely
to return the favor.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.950
A lot of people have been in
a situation or a conversation

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.320
where somebody starts
talking about their dreams

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.010
and their ambitions and their skills

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.450
and things they've done.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.390
And then the other person just
responds with their dreams

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.540
and their ambitions and the
things that they want to do.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.290
And they sort of just start,
it's like a back and forth,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.550
they're trying to one up each other,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.410
and nobody really gets anything
out of the conversation,

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:27.750
no one's really listening.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.530
They're just focusing on basically,

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:31.670
you know what measuring.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.960
But when you actually listen to the person

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.450
talk about their ambitions
and their skills,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.840
and you genuinely ask
them pressing questions

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.400
about what their
motivation was behind this,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.920
what they think they got out
of it, et cetera, et cetera,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.280
and you actually practice
being genuinely curious,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.010
not only would that person
really appreciate the fact

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.040
that you're actually
asking genuine questions

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.350
and that you're listening,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.020
they're way more likely to
ask you genuine questions

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.000
about yourself.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.440
And you can generate a pretty
close friendship very quickly.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.710
So next time you dive into a
conversation with somebody new,

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:07.230
genuinely try to be an
investigator in what is going on

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.063
in their life.

00:05:08.063 --> 00:05:10.660
What is making them tick
and try to get to know them

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:11.780
and actually listen.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.550
Lesson number two, make them feel valued.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.190
Now, before I dive into this tip,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.510
I just want to say something.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.817
A lot of people think that this book,

00:05:19.817 --> 00:05:22.020
"How to Win Friends and Influence People",

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.870
and these tips that I'm giving,

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.070
and the tips that are
available in the book,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.710
that they're somehow manipulative.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.050
I don't see that as true.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.020
They're only manipulative

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.750
if you use them to manipulate somebody.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.600
Giving somebody a genuine compliment,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.530
or even if you make it a habit

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.310
to give someone a genuine
compliment only does good.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.540
Putting somebody in a good mood

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.190
by complimenting their
appearance, a personality trait,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.590
or something that you
genuinely find good about them

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.290
often makes their day and they walk around

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:50.910
the office or town or
whatever they're doing

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.730
with a kind of swagger in their step.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:55.480
All because you complimented
on something that you noticed

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.470
that you liked about them.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:59.250
One of the greatest ways you
can make people feel valued

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.187
is to use their name and use it liberally.

00:06:02.187 --> 00:06:03.717
"Hey Joey, how's it going?"

00:06:03.717 --> 00:06:05.900
"Oh, hey Joey." "Hey
guys, look, it's Joey."

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.910
That makes me feel great
when people use my name,

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.070
especially if they
don't know me very well,

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.560
or if I just met them maybe one time

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.060
and they remembered my
name, that makes my day,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.350
like it makes me feel valued,

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.280
it makes me feel like
they actually remembered

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.400
our conversation,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:20.750
even though they probably didn't.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.230
By using somebody's name,

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.440
it makes people feel like
you have a connection,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.440
just instantly.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.640
The second way to make people feel valued

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.930
is to give genuine compliments.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.660
Once you start practicing
empathy and start practicing

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.050
seeing things from another
person's perspective,

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.630
like the book is all about,

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.450
then you'll get really
good at noticing things

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.420
that stand out from person to person.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.030
And once you get in the habit
of vocalizing those things,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.410
you'll become somebody who
people gravitate towards,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.750
and people just really like being around.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.260
Once you start to become
an investigator in people,

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.100
you can really start
to psychoanalyze people

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.570
and compliment them on
their actual personality.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.730
Complimenting someone's
personality is often

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.240
way more powerful than just
complimenting something

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.600
that's hanging off their body, a necklace,

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.320
or that sounded like, that's a weird,

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.310
telling somebody that they're
"An extremely good listener

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.755
and I really appreciate it"

00:07:09.755 --> 00:07:11.477
is a lot more powerful than saying,

00:07:11.477 --> 00:07:12.400
"I like your shirt."

